<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Uroš V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1336">1336</a>–<a href="/years/1371">1371</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Uroš the Weak · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Uroš V was the second and last emperor of the Serbian Empire, ruling from 1355 to 1371 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>. During his reign the great nobles became practically independent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">Vukašin Mrnjavčević</a> was crowned king and co-ruler in 1365. He died childless in 1371, soon after the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a>, ending the Nemanjić line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v.jpg" alt="Stefan Uroš V" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UrosV.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Uros V was the only son of Stefan Dusan and Helena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</a>. He was crowned king, the second highest title, as heir and co-ruler in 1346, after Dusan was crowned emperor. By the time he became sole ruler and emperor in 1355 he was no longer a minor, but he remained heavily dependent on his mother and on various members of the court. In historiography and folk tradition he is known as Uros the Weak, and he is recognised as a saint by the Serbian Orthodox Church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the spring of 1356 Nikephoros Orsini landed on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and overran it, then drove the despot Simeon Uros, the emperor's paternal uncle, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>. Simeon retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and western Macedonia, seized Kostur and proclaimed himself tsar, hoping to share the throne or replace Uros. The state council at Skoplje rejected his claims, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>' endorsement Uros became more active and issued a number of charters. Simeon was defeated in 1358 when he attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a> region, and in 1359 he retook Epirus and Thessaly and ruled there as emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p>In the following years the empire fragmented into principalities, some of which did not even nominally acknowledge Uros. His mother Helena ruled autonomously from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> in alliance with Jovan Ugljesa, and the Dejanovic and Balsic families and Nikola Altomanovic acted in the same way. By 1365 the most powerful nobleman was Ugljesa's brother Vukasin Mrnjavcevic, who became co-ruler with the title of king. By 1369 Vukasin had named his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a> as heir, as Uros was childless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Uros died childless in December 1371, after the Turks had destroyed much of the Serbian nobility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> earlier that year. The cause of his death is unknown. He was canonised 211 years after his death, and his body is kept in the Jazak monastery on Fruska Gora.</p><p>Marko inherited his father's title, but real power in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> lay with Lazar Hrebeljanovic, who took no imperial or royal title. Serbian folklore and epic poems describe Uros as a just ruler of pleasant appearance but weak character, a view many historians share, although some argue that the decline began much later in his reign than is supposed. It was long held that Vukasin murdered him, but Vukasin was proven to have died before the emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Uroš V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperors were Stefan Dušan (1346–1355) and <b>Stefan Uroš V</b> (1355–1371); Vukašin of Serbia was co-ruler with the title of king from 1365, and Prince Marko was designated heir as young king from 1369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Vukašin Mrnjavčević, a Serbian noble, was crowned king in 1365 as co-ruler with Emperor <b>Uroš V</b> of the Serbian Empire, ruling lands in western Macedonia from Prilep.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li><li><blockquote>Nikola Altomanović, son of a nobleman in Stefan Dušan's service, built up lands in the 1360s as <b>Uroš V</b>'s authority faded.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-nikola-altomanovic">Lordship of Nikola Altomanović</a></li><li><blockquote>Under his son <b>Uroš V</b> the great nobles became effectively independent.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
